{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>See, I have called by name Betzalel.</b> And so Moses mentioned to Israel (Exodus 35:30) ראו קרא ה׳ בשם. The matter is that it was impossible by nature that there should be found among Israel any person who was skilled and complete in any craft in the world, since Israel were enslaved in Egypt with crushing labor and trained in mortar and bricks, and it was not to be expected that there should be found among them any person skilled in any delicate craft, for they had not seen it nor learned it from a teacher, nor was there among them anyone who had trained his hands in it. And even if there had been among them a wise man skilled in one craft, it would be expected that he would forget it from being occupied with the work of mortar and bricks. And when there would be among them one who is wise, complete, and skilled in one delicate and fine craft — and all the more so if he were skilled and complete in all the crafts: in silver and in gold and in bronze and in stone-cutting and in wood-cutting, and in designing and embroidery and weaving — this is a great wonder. And still more, that he should be complete in wisdom and in understanding and in knowledge — all this was found in Betzalel. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: Betzalel knew how to combine the letters with which heaven and earth were created. It is written here: ואמלא אותו רוח אלהים בחכמה ובתבונה ובדעת, and it is written there (Proverbs 3:19–20): ״ה׳ בחכמה יסד ארץ כונן שמים בתבונה, בדעתו תהומות נבקעו.״ Therefore it says ראה קראתי בשם, that is: See this great wonder — that I call the generations from the beginning, and I have called by name Betzalel son of Uri son of Hur, to grasp the secret of the Tabernacle and its vessels and to engage in its work. And the sense of בשם בצלאל, according to its plain sense, is 'by the name of Betzalel.' But there is in this a hint, that בשם is the Name, may He be exalted, as in (Exodus 33:17) ואדעך בשם. And it comes to hint that Betzalel — and likewise all the other prophets — were emanated by the great Name." "<b>And to this</b> the Midrash alluded when they said: The Holy One, blessed be He, showed Moses the book of the first man, and even Betzalel — from that hour I prepared him, as it says ראה קראתי בשם בצלאל. This comes to hint that Betzalel was 'in the Name,' that is, from that hour when all the generations and all created beings were emanated from Wisdom; and so the words בשם בצלאל indicate, whether with regard to the Holy One, blessed be He, or with regard to Moses. And this is clear, for Betzalel was so called because at first his soul was inscribed and engraved in the power of the emanation of El, and afterward he went forth and merited and dwelt בצל אל — in the shadow of El — to make the Ark, which was the shadow of the written Torah, and the cover with its cherubim spreading their wings, which were a shadow over the Ark; and from the six days of Creation this was prepared for him." "<b>And further</b> in the Midrash: ראה קראתי בשם — and Moses said ראו. 'See' is in the upper realms; 'see' is in the lower realms — as it is written (Proverbs 3:4): ומצא חן ושכל טוב בעיני אלהים ואדם. Thus they interpreted in Tanchuma." "<b>And know</b> that Betzalel at the work of the Tabernacle was thirteen years old. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in Sanhedrin, chapter Ben Sorer uMoreh: It is written here ובצלאל בן אורי בן חור, and it is written (I Chronicles 2:19) ותמת עזובה ויקח לו כלב את אפרת ותלד לו את חור. And did Betzalel make the Tabernacle at thirteen years of age? It is written (Exodus 36:4) איש איש ממלאכתו אשר המה עושים. And it was taught: the first year Moses made the Tabernacle; the second he erected it and sent the spies, as it is written (Joshua 14:6–7) בן ארבעים שנה אנכי בשלוח משה עבד ה׳ אותי מקדש ברנע. How many is forty? Subtract fourteen, which was Betzalel's age; twenty-six remain, for two or three years of pregnancy are found, since each and every one gave birth at eight." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ראה קראתי בשם בצלאל — the Holy One, blessed be He, showed Moses the book of the first man and said to him: each and every one I prepared from that hour; and even Betzalel — from that hour I prepared him, as it says ראה קראתי בשם בצלאל."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:06:04.278233419Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}